### name: stmt.take.count-uint64-max-is-clamped.mariadb
--- note
MariaDB accepts 2^64-1 (and uses it itself as the offset-only sentinel, 28-slice-overflow.sqlt), but a user count is clamped like everywhere else: the clamp is by DIALECT-INDEPENDENT rule, so a rule translates to the same count on every target.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"ITEMS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "items"}}
--- as
statement
--- source
ITEMS .> TAKE(18446744073709551615)
--- expect
SELECT * FROM `items` LIMIT 9223372036854775807
===

### name: stmt.drop.count-past-int64-is-clamped.mariadb
--- note
The sentinel LIMIT 18446744073709551615 in front of an OFFSET is MariaDB's spelling of 'no limit', not a user count, and the clamp never touches it.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"ITEMS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "items"}}
--- as
statement
--- source
ITEMS .> DROP(18446744073709551616)
--- expect
SELECT * FROM `items` LIMIT 18446744073709551615 OFFSET 9223372036854775807
===

